WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.016
Deliberate practice starts with a clear skill definition.

00:00:03.016 --> 00:00:07.390
A vague ambition like get better is not enough because it does

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:09.212
not say what to practice,

00:00:09.212 --> 00:00:13.586
how to practice it, or how to know whether progress is happening.

00:00:13.586 --> 00:00:17.595
A clearer target might be identifying root cause from log evidence,

00:00:17.595 --> 00:00:20.511
writing concise executive summaries, asking better diagnostic questions,

00:00:20.511 --> 00:00:23.427
or explaining technical risk to a nontechnical audience.

00:00:23.427 --> 00:00:26.707
Complex work usually needs to be broken into subskills.

00:00:26.707 --> 00:00:29.987
Troubleshooting, for example, may include forming hypotheses, collecting evidence,

00:00:29.987 --> 00:00:33.268
testing assumptions, communicating status, and deciding when to escalate.

00:00:33.268 --> 00:00:37.277
Writing may include organizing ideas, choosing the right level of detail,

00:00:37.277 --> 00:00:39.828
making claims precise, and revising for clarity.

00:00:39.828 --> 00:00:44.931
Skill decomposition turns a broad capability into smaller parts that can actually be practiced.

00:00:44.931 --> 00:00:47.483
Observable behaviors make practice easier to design.

00:00:47.483 --> 00:00:52.221
If the goal is better incident handoffs, the behavior might be summarizing impact,

00:00:52.221 --> 00:00:55.501
current hypothesis, evidence gathered, decisions made, and next action.

00:00:55.501 --> 00:00:58.053
If the goal is better code review,

00:00:58.053 --> 00:01:02.791
the behavior might be identifying the security-relevant data flow before commenting on style.

00:01:02.791 --> 00:01:06.071
Good success criteria describe what strong performance looks like.

00:01:06.071 --> 00:01:09.351
Choosing one improvement target at a time protects focus.

00:01:09.351 --> 00:01:12.632
If a learner tries to improve everything at once,

00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:15.183
feedback becomes noisy and cognitive load rises.

00:01:15.183 --> 00:01:18.463
Start with the bottleneck that most limits real-world performance.

00:01:18.463 --> 00:01:22.837
Practice it deliberately, measure the change, then move to the next subskill.

00:01:22.837 --> 00:01:27.940
This keeps practice connected to capability instead of becoming a collection of disconnected exercises.
